<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karl Philipp von Wrede</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1767">1767</a>–<a href="/years/1838">1838</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Prince Wrede</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Karl Philipp von Wrede was a Bavarian field marshal. He commanded Bavarian troops alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, including in the Russian campaign of 1812, then negotiated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ried">Treaty of Ried</a> in 1813 by which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>. He led an Austro-Bavarian army that failed to stop Napoleon's retreat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hanau">Battle of Hanau</a>, and represented Bavaria at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karl-philipp-von-wrede.jpg" alt="Karl Philipp von Wrede" width="320" height="459" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Franz Hanfstaengl · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karl_Philipp_Wrede.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Wrede was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> on 29 April 1767, the youngest of three children of Ferdinand Josef Wrede, who was made Baron von Wrede in 1791, and Anna Katharina Jünger. He was educated for a career as a civil official in the government of the Electorate of the Palatinate. When the campaign of 1799 began, he raised a volunteer corps in the Palatinate and became its colonel. The well-drilled Austrians beside whom it served at first laughed at it, but Wrede soon brought it into good order, and it distinguished itself during Kray's retreat on Ulm.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He commanded a Palatinate infantry brigade at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hohenlinden">Battle of Hohenlinden</a>, and after the peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneville">Lunéville</a> he became a lieutenant-general in the Bavarian Army. He fought against the Austrian invasion of 1805 and distinguished himself at the Battle of Pułtusk, though French treatment of the Bavarian troops and accusations of looting against him strained relations, which only the tact of King Maximilian of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> kept calm in 1807 and 1809.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-fifth-coalition">War of the Fifth Coalition</a> he led the 2nd Bavarian Division. He played an important part at the Battle of Abensberg on 20 April 1809, took 11 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landshut">Landshut</a> the next day and was defeated at Neumarkt-Sankt Veit on 24 April. He then moved against the Tyrolean Rebellion and helped crush the division of Johann Gabriel Chasteler de Courcelles at the Battle of Wörgl on 13 May. At Wagram on 6 July his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavarians">Bavarians</a> supported Jacques MacDonald's attack, and Wrede, grazed by a bullet during the charge on Sussenbrunn, briefly believed himself fatally wounded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Wrede commanded the Bavarian corps in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1812 and, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> changed sides, fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> at Hanau on 30 and 31 October 1813, where Napoleon smashed one of his poorly placed wings and inflicted 9,000 casualties. In 1814 he led the attack on the French left flank at La Rothière under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gebhard-leberecht-von-blucher">Blücher</a>, and that year he was made prince and field marshal. He had married Countess Sofie von Wiser-Siegelsbach in 1795, and they had eight children. He died at Ellingen on 12 December 1838.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Karl Philipp von Wrede” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Treaty of Ried, General <b>Karl Philipp von Wrede</b> tried to block Napoleon's retreat but was defeated at the Battle of Hanau in October 1813.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Ellingen, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.41,8.7&amp;year=1813" aria-label="Heidelberg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4750 442 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Karl Philipp von Wrede"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5200 712h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5200 712h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.41,8.7&amp;year=1813">What was here in 1813? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
